; Windmill — NEW farm-building placeable (content-variety pass Task 3, 2026-08-26), one of
; 8 brand-new PlaceableDefinition entries. See project/data/buildings/windmill.tres for
; the full data-entry header (proposed cost/footprint, human sign-off flag).
;
; Source: Quaternius, "Farm Buildings" pack (FBX), Windmill.fbx — CC0 1.0 Universal (this
; pack also ships a taller "TowerWindmill.fbx"; the brief's table specifies Windmill.fbx,
; used here — TowerWindmill is a separate, unevaluated candidate). License.txt confirmed
; CC0 1.0 Universal / Public Domain Dedication before import, per
; asset-import-pipeline.md's audit gate. Same NEW pack as Barn.tscn — see
; project/attribution/sources/quaternius_farm_buildings.tres.
;
; TEXTURE AUDIT (mandatory — DeadTree/PineTree2 precedent; see Barn.tscn's header for the
; full method): probed every surface on the raw, unmodified FBX (both the "Windmill" base
; node and the "Windmill_Blades" rotor node). albedo_texture null on every surface, but
; vertex_color_use_as_albedo reads true with MEANINGFUL, DISTINCT per-surface colors
; (brown wood ~(0.5115, 0.3069, 0.2132), mid grey ~(0.6269, 0.6269, 0.6269)) — same
; pack's named flat-color materials baked into real per-vertex color, NOT the uniform
; near-(0.9063, 0.9063, 0.9063, 1.0) missing-texture tell. No texture fix needed; no
; material_override applied. Plain instance.
;
; SCALE: raw AABB measured via manual local-transform composition on the unmodified FBX
; (NOT global_transform) — this measurement is the COMBINED base+blades AABB, so the
; blades (which project sideways, well past the base tower) set the x axis: footprint
; 6.5077 x 1.8979 (x, z) tile-units, height 11.1962. Target max-axis 0.869309 (House's own
; established 1x1 margin). Long axis (x, the blade span) scaled by 0.133582. Scaled
; result: 0.8693 x 1.4956 x 0.2535 (x, y, z) — a tall, narrow tower with wide-spanning
; blades, all capped to the blade-tip extent. FIRST-PASS PROPOSAL, not a measured GDD
; number: capping scale to the BLADE SPAN (rather than the narrower tower-only footprint,
; which would let the tower itself sit larger on the tile with blades overhanging the
; neighbor the way tree canopies overhang their slab elsewhere in this project) is a
; judgment call — FLAG FOR HUMAN SIGN-OFF, specifically on whether the resulting tower
; (visually slim inside a 1x1 tile once the blade extent is priced in) reads correctly, or
; whether an overhang treatment (blade-span uncapped, tower-only footprint driving the
; scale instead) would look better.
;
; FACING: left as authored (identity transform, no rotation) — unconfirmed by a human eye
; pass. FLAG FOR HUMAN SIGN-OFF.
; RECENTRED 2026-09-07 (farm-buildings centring pass), and this is a CORRECTION, not a new
; judgment call. MEASURED: this wrapper's world-composed mesh AABB centred on (+0.132851, _,
; -0.000000) in the wrapper's own space rather than on (0, 0) — the mesh is off-centre on its own
; origin in the source asset. A building is positioned by its TILE CENTRE, so an off-centre mesh
; sits off-centre on its plot and eats into its margin on one side.
;
; The `Model` node's translation below is exactly the negation of that measured centre
; (-0.132851, 0, +0.000000) — a number read off the mesh, not chosen, so nothing here needs a
; human ruling. X and Z only: Y is untouched because the model already sits on the ground plane
; and vertical placement is not what a footprint reserves. THE SCALE FACTOR IS UNCHANGED —
; recentring moves the mesh, it does not resize it, so every fit decision this file already
; records still stands exactly as written.
;
; WHY THIS SURFACED NOW: `test_building_footprint_alignment.gd` used to place only `barn` and
; `house`, so eight of the ten buildables were never checked against the tiles they reserve. The
; 2026-09-07 house cull hit the same defect in three House wrappers, which prompted auditing the
; rest; that suite now covers EVERY buildable, so this class of defect cannot return unnoticed.
; CORRECTION TO THIS FILE'S OWN SCALE NOTE, and the one real spill in the catalog:
; THIS ONE WAS ACTUALLY SPILLING: the mesh ran to x=+0.5675 against a half-tile of 0.500,
; so 0.0675 of it hung over the neighbouring tile. Centring is what fixes that, and the
; per-node measurement corrects this file's own header note in passing. The header says the
; blades "project sideways, well past the base tower" and so "set the x axis" — measured,
; only half of that is true. `Windmill_Blades` spans x[-0.3018, +0.3018] and is ALREADY
; centred; the `Windmill` tower body spans x[-0.1364, +0.5675]. The blades set the MINUS
; side, the TOWER sets the plus side, and it is the tower that overhung. Centring the tower
; instead (offset -0.2156) would push the blades to -0.5174 and simply move the spill to the
; other side, so centring the combined envelope is not a preference here — it is the only
; spill-free placement this rigid mesh allows. NOTE the blades are consequently no longer
; centred on the tile (they sit at x[-0.4347, +0.1690]); if the header's own open question
; about an overhang treatment is ever answered the other way, revisit this offset with it.
;
; RESCALED 2026-09-08 — FOOTPRINT-DEEPENING TRIAL (human ruling: "double everything uniformly").
; The eight 1x1 buildables moved to a 2x2 footprint, so the long-horizontal-axis normalisation
; target doubles: 0.869309 -> 1.738618 — the target Barn.tscn's own 2x2 already uses, so this is
; the project's existing 2x2 convention, not a new one. This file's basis scale AND its
; recentring translation are therefore each multiplied by exactly 2 (0.133582 -> 0.267164).
;
; DOUBLING RATHER THAN RE-MEASURING IS DELIBERATE. A uniform factor of 2 preserves every ratio
; already ruled on, so nothing decided earlier is silently reverted — the SCALE and RECENTRED
; notes above still describe their own derivation correctly, at exactly half this file's current
; numbers. The translation doubles for the same reason the scale does: it is the negation of a
; centre measured in post-scale wrapper space, so it scales with the mesh.
;
; THIS IS A TRIAL FOR A LOOK-PASS, NOT A SIGNED-OFF VALUE. The footprint change it serves is
; unratified (spec.md Open Question #18) and the downstream re-tuning it implies is deliberately
; NOT done yet — see the buildable's own .tres header for the outstanding list.
;
; WHY: buildings read too small against a person. `Man.tscn` normalises a villager to exactly
; 1.0 tile height (WorldGrid.TILE_SIZE = 1.0, gdd.md -> "a villager stands ~1 tile tall"), and
; every 1x1 building's scaled mesh stood SHORTER than that — all three House looks (0.772,
; 0.909, 0.706), the Small Barn (0.687) shorter than the 0.734 cow, the Open Barn/stable (0.667)
; shorter than the 0.965 horse. The 0.869309 target was a convention inherited from the FirstAge
; House batch; it was never measured against a person.
;
